  "headline": "Copper, oil and gold sold while the long bond rallies: a growth scare, not my squeeze",
  "body": [
    "Today was not a margin call, and that is the problem. Stocks slipped 0.2% and the VIX eased to 24.6, well off the top of its range. Yet copper fell 4.6% and Brent 4.2%. Gold made another yearly low as the 30-year rallied 4.6 basis points. Hard assets sold into a calmer tape with the long bond bought is a growth scare in which the Fed is believed, and it runs straight against my thesis.",
    "Yesterday I called a squeeze and leaned on a long bond that refused to rally. Today it rallied harder than the 5-year, and the 30-year minus 5-year spread narrowed to 53 basis points. The bond market calmed too, MOVE back under 100 at 98. My crack condition was gold falling as the dollar fell and the VIX eased; two of the three fired. The dollar's 0.3% rise explains a sliver of gold's 2.3% drop, and the rest is a bid that was not there.",
    "The longer horizons are worse for me. Over three months the 5-year is up 53 basis points and the 30-year only 22, a bear flattening that prices a Fed holding firm, not one bending to the Treasury. Stocks down 5% over the same stretch is the one piece that still fits fiscal dominance, but it fits a Fed that is too tight at least as well. Bitcoin in the bottom tenth of its range, buying 17 ounces of gold and down 14% on the month, is a risk asset losing to a collapsing metal.",
    "The oil channel confirms it. Drop Site reports Israel launched a ground operation in central Gaza, a day after MintPress had the IRGC striking a helicopter base in Bahrain, and Brent fell 4% anyway, down 19% on the month. The rupee firmed 0.5% while USD/CAD printed a new high. The importer is rewarded and the exporter pays, so the market prices supply as ample and demand as the problem. The accelerant is going out, not firing.",
    "My book is unchanged: dollar cash, out of gold, the 30-year, Brent and bitcoin. But the thesis is now on a clock, not in a timing problem. Gold comes back on the first close higher with the VIX also higher, and through $4,000 with a softer dollar confirms it. I retract the fiscal-dominance read if the 30-year minus 5-year spread drops below 45 basis points with MOVE below 90, a long end bought and a bond market pacified; then this is a tight-Fed regime and I own the dollar alone. I am wrong about today being a growth scare if the VIX is back above 30 with gold still falling, which would mean yesterday's squeeze read was right and I called its end early."
  ],
